{"name":"Flattery","hex":"#6b4424","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Flattery","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A surname from Irish."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Flattery"}],"entries":[{"title":"Flattery","blocks":[{"text":"Flattery, also called adulation or blandishment, is the act of giving excessive compliments, generally for the purpose of ingratiating oneself with the subject. It is also used in pick-up lines when attempting to initiate romantic courtship.\nHistorically, flattery has been used as a standard form of discourse when addressing a king or queen. In the Renaissance, it was a common practice among writers to flatter the reigning monarch, as Edmund Spenser flattered Queen Elizabeth I in The Faerie Queene, William Shakespeare flattered King James I in Macbeth, Niccolò Machiavelli flattered Lorenzo II de' Medici in The Prince, and Jean de La Fontaine flattered Louis XIV of France in his Fables."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/df/Brooklyn_Museum_-_Yes_It_Is_My_Deceased_Wife...Only_You_Have_Flattered_Her_Too_Much_-_Honor%C3%A9_Daumier.jpg/330px-Brooklyn_Museum_-_Yes_It_Is_My_Deceased_Wife...Only_You_Have_Flattered_Her_Too_Much_-_Honor%C3%A9_Daumier.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flattery","wikidata":"Q2918702"},{"title":"Flattery (film)","blocks":[{"text":"Flattery is a 1925 American silent drama film directed by Tom Forman and starring John Bowers, Marguerite De La Motte, and Alan Hale."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2b/Flattery_%28film%29.jpg/330px-Flattery_%28film%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flattery_(film)","wikidata":"Q3746422"}],"images":[],"sounds":{},"pictures":[{"title":"Vase with lamps, Brown and beige textures, Rostov-on-Don, Russia","image":"https://th